From efc734ecb69faaabda50ff721d0dcc0494d8e255 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 17:30:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Count the session's changes, not the branch's divergence
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The session-list chip was computed from the branch changeset in two places.
A branch measures itself against its upstream, so it includes commits made
before the session existed and by other people; a session that never ran a
turn still got a count.

Ownership moves to the session changeset on the write path, and the read
path's branch-derived fallback — which also persisted what it derived,
making the wrong number durable — is removed. With no session-scoped
evidence the chip is absent, and the next completed turn writes the real one.

Fixing either half alone is inert: the write path persists the branch number
and the read path then serves it faithfully from the stored summary.

Session diffs come from checkpoints, so a session without them now shows no
chip rather than a wrong one.
