 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vs/platform/agentHost/test/node/agentHostChangesetService.test.ts b/src/vs/platform/agentHost/test/node/agentHostChangesetService.test.ts
index 8a4d3c588a925..b89320f895884 100644
--- a/src/vs/platform/agentHost/test/node/agentHostChangesetService.test.ts
+++ b/src/vs/platform/agentHost/test/node/agentHostChangesetService.test.ts
@@ -1996,6 +1996,13 @@ suite('AgentHostChangesetService - multi-root turn changeset', () => {
 
 			svc.refreshBranchChangeset(sessionStr);
 			await waitForCount(() => stateManager.getChangesetState(buildBranchChangesetUri(sessionStr))?.status === ChangesetStatus.Ready ? 1 : 0, 1);
+			// `waitForCount` gives up silently, so assert what it was waiting for
+			// rather than letting a timeout surface as a confusing later failure.
+			assert.strictEqual(
+				stateManager.getChangesetState(buildBranchChangesetUri(sessionStr))?.status,
+				ChangesetStatus.Ready,
+				'branch changeset did not become ready',
+			);
 
 			// The branch changeset itself is still published — only its ownership
 			// of the chip is withdrawn.

From bad496d34f054d43c4d02613e11b691d90f4f023 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 13:22:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Keep branch divergence out of the legacy session key, and
 fix the build

Review raised that this change stops the chip being derived from branch
divergence, yet the branch refresh still mirrors its diffs into the legacy
`'diffs'` key. That key is documented as the session-wide list and, more to
the point, `parsePersistedStaticChangesets` parses it as the SESSION
changeset and seeds it into session state. So branch-vs-upstream diffs
written there came back on restore as the session's own work: the same
mislabelling this PR removes from the chip, arriving by a different route.

The mirror now happens on the session path, where the payload matches what
the key means and what reads it. Its comment already claimed to write "the
session-changeset payload"; it was simply on the wrong branch of the
conditional.

Also drops the `ChangesetState` type import, which this PR left unused when
it deleted `computeChangesSummaryFromLiveState`. It failed `noUnusedLocals`,
so the branch did not compile.
